  "kent": {
    "source": {
      "title": "Words of Estimative Probability",
      "authors": "Sherman Kent",
      "publication": "Studies in Intelligence, vol. 8, no. 4 (CIA), 1964; approved for release 22 September 1993",
      "date": "1964",
      "url": "https://www.cia.gov/resources/csi/static/Words-of-Estimative-Probability.pdf",
      "licence": "Work of the United States government (public domain)",
      "measured": "Nothing was measured: Kent proposed numerical odds for five estimative phrases used in National Intelligence Estimates, plus lists of accepted synonyms.",
      "transformation": "The chart's centre and 'give or take' values are copied as printed; lower = centre - give or take and upper = centre + give or take. Synonyms inherit the range of the phrase they are listed under. Bracketed alternatives are split: 'odds [or chances] overwhelming' becomes two phrases, 'chances a little better [or less]' becomes two, and 'we doubt, doubtful' becomes two. 'Probably' and 'almost certainly' are the adverb forms Kent himself uses for 'probable' and 'almost certain' (footnote 9 and the 'probably'/'we believe' bracket). The 'possible' group carries no odds in the essay, so it is shown without numbers."
    },
